What is cgedit
==============

cgedit is a simple text editor, as Leafpad is: a window with menus and
the text and nothing else, one file to a window. It is written for
CGTK. It does what Leafpad does and looks as Leafpad looks, but only
the finding out of codesets is Leafpad's code; the rest is new, as
Leafpad stands on the text widget of GTK 2 and there is nothing like
that widget here.

What it has, which is what Leafpad has:

  - files in other codesets than the locale's, read and written, with
    the codeset found out on opening or chosen in the file selector,
    and line ends that are LF, CR+LF or CR, kept as they were found
  - undo and redo without limit
  - find, find next and previous, and replace, a place at a time or
    all at once, with every place found shown up
  - jump to a line
  - a font of the user's choosing, word wrap, line numbers, and auto
    indent, kept from one time to the next with the window's size
  - a tab or Shift and a tab to set selected lines further in or back
  - files and text taken when dropped on it, and text dragged out
  - the options --codeset, --tab-width and --jump

The menus, their keys and the questions asked are Leafpad's.


The editor widget
=================

The toolkit's own text widget, GtkText, is as it was in GTK 1.2: it
has Emacs's keys built in, which a menu's keys cannot take from it,
and it does not undo. cgedit does not use it. It has a widget of its
own, GtkEditor, in gtkeditor.c, over a text of its own, in textbuf.c,
which knows nothing of the toolkit.

The text is UTF-8 with a gap where it was last changed, and an index
of where each line starts. A file of a million lines opens in about a
second and is then typed in as a short one is.

The widget is written as one of the toolkit's would be, to the
toolkit's 1.4.0 as it was released and with nothing added to it, so
that it could go into the toolkit one day as it is. Its keys are the
usual ones and no others, and a key it has no use for it leaves to the
window, so a menu can have any key.

It draws a character after another from left to right, as everything
in the toolkit does. Writing that goes from right to left, or whose
letters join or stack, is not laid out as it should be. Leafpad had
Pango for that.


What is not as Leafpad has it
=============================

It does not print: nothing in the toolkit prints. Leafpad was built
without printing too, where there was nothing to print with.

A file that starts with the mark of UTF-16 is read as that. Leafpad
did not look for it.

Control+T, which keeps the window above the others, asks the desktop
for that; under Wayland there is nothing to ask.

It is not translated yet: it has its text domain and cgedit/po, with
no catalogues in it.


Building
========

See the README at the top of cgtools.


Use
===

  cgedit [--codeset=CODESET] [--tab-width=WIDTH] [--jump=LINE] [FILE]

The settings are kept in cgedit/cgeditrc, in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME or in
~/.config.

The man page, cgedit(1), has the menus and the keys.
